{
  "id": "kubectl_find_pods_with_env",
  "type": "command",
  "question": "What fields in the Container specification define environment variables, and how do EnvVar and EnvFromSource differ in how they inject values into a container?",
  "resource": "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Container",
  "scope": "Namespaced",
  "multi_hop": true,
  "ground_truth": {
    "answer": "The Container spec provides two distinct mechanisms for environment variable injection. The env field is a list of EnvVar objects, where each entry defines a single variable by name. The value can be a literal string, or it can come from one of four sources defined by EnvVarSource: a specific key in a ConfigMap (configMapKeyRef), a specific key in a Secret (secretKeyRef), a Pod or Node metadata field such as the pod name or namespace (fieldRef — the Downward API), or a container's own resource limit or request value (resourceFieldRef — also Downward API). This makes env the only mechanism that can inject cluster and pod introspection data into a container. The envFrom field, by contrast, is a list of EnvFromSource objects that bulk-inject all keys from a single ConfigMap or Secret as environment variables, with an optional prefix string. It cannot target individual keys, cannot access Downward API data, and cannot inject literal values — it is exclusively a bulk-import mechanism for external configuration objects.",
    "context": [
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Container: env is a list of EnvVar objects; envFrom is a list of EnvFromSource for bulk injection from ConfigMap or Secret",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.EnvVar: name is required; value is a literal string; valueFrom is an EnvVarSource for dynamic references",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.EnvVarSource: configMapKeyRef, secretKeyRef, fieldRef, resourceFieldRef are the four source types",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.EnvFromSource: configMapRef or secretRef specifies the bulk source; prefix is an optional string prepended to all injected key names"
    ],
